Year = {2003},
Volume = {1250},
Pages = {3-14},
Note = {International Symposium in Limbic and Association Cortical Systems,
   Toyama, JAPAN, OCT 07-12, 2002},
Abstract = {The somatosensory association cortices we refer to here are those areas
   in the caudal postcentral gyrus and in the frontoparietal operculum, the
   second somatosensory cortex. In a series of single unit studies in
   monkeys, we have demonstrated that the complexity of receptive field
   properties increases along the rostrocaudal axis of the postcentral
   gyms, and proposed a hierarchical scheme for information processing in
   this gyrus. The hierarchical scheme has led us to find more complex
   types of somatosensory integration in the intraparietal region:
   integration of bilateral sides of the body, and that of somatosensory
   and visual information take place in the caudal-most part of the
   postcentral gyrus. Recent brain imaging studies in humans confirmed the
   presence of hierarchical processing in the postcentral gyrus. Also,
   studies in monkeys and those performed in human subjects have suggested
   that the caudal part of the postcentral gyrus is one of the key stations
   for perception of the peripersonal space and for the formation of body
   image. We further studied neuronal receptive field properties in the
   second somatosensory cortex (SII). There, neurons with bilateral
   receptive fields are in the majority. Hands and arms are dominant.
   Remote and discontinuous body regions were integrated in various
   combinations. These results support the notion that SII is a station
   higher than the postcentral regions. (C) 2003 Published by Elsevier B.V.},
