Note = {International Conference on Social Sciences and Education (ICSSE),
   Bandung, INDONESIA, NOV 21-23, 2017},
Abstract = {In the customer service sector, emotional labour is an integral part of
   service providers' job in order to uphold service quality and ensure
   customer satisfaction and loyalty. Due to the demands of their job,
   literature shows that service providers suffer from emotional
   dissonance, role stress and role conflict. When emotional labour is
   practiced either through surface acting or deep acting, latent conflict
   is born. Latent conflict refers to moments when conflict is supposed to
   occur but does not. By exhibiting emotional labour in reaction to
   (mis)behaviours, service providers may be encouraging such
   (mis)behaviours in customers. This paper addresses the occurrence of
   latent impoliteness by using the qualitative approach of Hermeneutic
   Phenomenology. Perceptions of impoliteness were investigated from the
   point of view of counter service providers in this case study of an
   International Office at a premier university in Malaysia through
   observation, reflective written exercises and in-depth semi-structured
   interviews. Findings show that in service encounters, impoliteness
   partly happens because service providers suppressed reactions that they
   perceived to be impolite. When the impolite reaction is finally
   displayed, this manifested impoliteness is termed as latent
   impoliteness, coined to refer to impoliteness that has been suppressed,
   due to latent conflict. Identification of the triggers of latent
   impoliteness would assist management to prevent or limit customer
   service deviance before they occur.},
