   PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY 18-20, 2005},
Organization = {Int Water Assoc},
Abstract = {This paper describes the moving bed biofilm reactor (MBBR) and presents
   applications of wastewater treatment processes in which this reactor is
   used. The MBBR processes have been extensively used for BOD/COD-removal,
   as well as for nitrification and denitrification in municipal and
   industrial wastewater treatment. This paper focuses on the municipal
   applications. The most frequent process combinations are presented and
   discussed. Basic design data obtained through research, as well as data
   from practical operation of various plants, are presented. It is
   demonstrated that the MBBR may be used in an extremely compact high-rate
   process (< 1 h total HRT) for secondary treatment. Most European plants
   require P-removal and performance data from plants combining MBBR and
   chemical precipitation is presented. Likewise, data from plants in Italy
   and Switzerland that are implementing nitrification in addition to
   secondary treatment are presented. The results from three Norwegian
   plants that are using the so-called combined denitrification MBBR
   process are discussed. Nitrification rates as high as 1.2g NH4-N/m(2) d
   at complete nitrification were demonstrated in practical operation at
   low temperatures (11 C), while denitrification rates were as high as 3.5
   g NO3 N-equiv/m(2).d. Depending on the extent of pretreatment, the total
   HRT of the MBBR for N-removal will be in the range of 3 to 5 h.},
