Note = {16th International Multidisciplinary Scientific Geoconference (SGEM
   2016), Albena, BULGARIA, JUN 30-JUL 06, 2016},
Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci; Natl
   Acad Sci Ukraine; Inst Water Problem \& Hydropower NAS KR; Natl Acad Sci
   Armenia; Sci Council Japan; World Acad Sci; European Acad Sci Arts \&
   Lett; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad Sci \& Arts; Croatian Acad Sci
   \& Arts; Georgian Natl Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Turkish Acad Sci; Bulgarian Ind Assoc; Bulgarian Minist Environm \&
   Water},
Abstract = {The decomposition and mineralization process of organic substances from
   sediment and water have a very important role in the aquatic
   environment, because it releases the mineral substances necessary to
   primer producers. Nine sediment and water samples had been collected
   from the eastern part of River Jiu (SW Romania). The samples were
   physically, chemically, bacterially and enzymatically studied. Four
   enzymatic activities (catalase, actual dehydrogenase and potential
   dehydrogenase, urease) had been determined, three ecophysiological
   groups of bacteria (aerobic and anaerobic nitrogen fixing bacteria,
   sulfate-reducing and iron-reducing bacteria) and eleven physical and
   chemical parameters were determined. From the analyzed samples we
   noticed an increase in Petrila downstream at fixed residues. Enzymatic
   activities have increased near the cities. Positive correlations have
   been found between the concentrations of NH4+ and the actual
   dehydrogenase activity (r=0.721, p=0.014) between NO2- and ADA (r=0.799,
   p=0.005), between PO4- and ADA (r=0.801, p=0.005). This shows us that
   the nutrient intake in water implicitly leads to an increased
   dehydrogenase activity in the sediment. High values are also recorded by
   EISQ, in samples taken near the cities. This is due to the nutrient
   intake coming from wastewater discharges in Petrila and Petrosani. The
   bacterial indicator is loyally following the enzymatic indicator and
   they have the same variations. Based on these data, the similarity
   between the collected samples was determined. The nutrient intake leads
   to an increased dehydrogenase activity, reflected by positive
   correlations between the nutrient concentrations and the enzymatic
   activity. EISQ increases near Petrila, due to domestic and industrial
   wastewater coming from human activities which brings an ample supply of
   nutrients. A higher similarity has been observed between samples taken
   outside the cities and the samples taken near the cities, indicating a
   strong anthropogenic influence, in the latter case. All analyzed
   enzymatic activities and ecophysiological groups of bacteria showed
   variations, according to the location of sampling point and anthropic
   influences due to human activity.},
