Pages = {756-761},
Note = {Applied Energy Symposium and Forum - Low-Carbon Cities and Urban Energy
   Systems (CUE), Shanghai, PEOPLES R CHINA, JUN 05-07, 2018},
Organization = {Appl Energy; Appl Energy Innovat Inst; Tongji Univ; China Assoc Sci \&
   Technol, HOME Program; Malardalen Univ, Future Energy Profile; Shanghai
   Key Lab Urban Regenerat \& Spatial Optimizat Technol; MOE Joint Lab Int
   Cooperat Eco Urban Design; Sichuan Univ; Shanghai Univ Electric Power;
   BEEUE; Int Clean Energy Talent Program; UNiLAB},
Abstract = {Climate change and urbanization are the main causes of regional climate
   change. Based on the measured meteorological data of Nanjing's daily
   average temperature and total electricity consumption in recent years,
   the method of degree days is used to estimate the demand for heating in
   winter and refrigeration in summer due to climate change. Moreover, we
   analyze the quantitative relationship between the degree days of
   heating/cooling and social electricity load requirements. The results
   reveal the heating degree days in winter increase first and then
   decrease, and the cooling degree days in summer decrease first and then
   increase during the period of 2011-2017. The air conditioning days in
   summer is most closely related to the total electricity consumption and
   the coefficient of correlation is above 0.7. While, the linear
   correlation between the number of air conditioning days and the total
   electricity consumption in the winter season is not significant. The
   correlation coefficient is only 0.2. It's obviously the change of degree
   days is closely related to the temperature change. As the typical
   representative place of the hot summer and cold winter regions, the
   average temperature of Nanjing continues to rise in recent years. This
   also confirms that more electricity is used at higher temperatures in
   summer due to refrigeration and air conditioning. Correspondingly,
   electricity consumption via space heating is decreased due to higher
   temperature in winner. Copyright (C) 2018 Elsevier Ltd. All rights
   reserved.},
