   System Science, Henan Polytechn Univ, Jiaozuo, PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY
   15-17, 2008},
Abstract = {Studies of China's energy development and carbon trading opportunities
   under CDM using aggregate approaches often face two kinds of
   difficulties: regional variations and institutional constraints of
   economy. This study initiates research that disaggregates the recent
   development of the Chinese electricity industry in a case study of Hubei
   Province. We highlight a bifurcated development of efficient generation
   technologies and link it to the institutional arrangements for
   electricity supply, change of fuel structure and specific policy
   incentives. We also find that carbon emission from the Hubei electricity
   generation follows a declining trend. An attempt to derive a shadow
   price of carbon by correlating heat rates with unit costs of generation
   shows some problems in the cost data. Our study suggests that credible
   and cost-effective baselines in Chinese power will demand desegregations
   along vectors of regions and technologies, and require an understanding
   of economic and institution characteristics of energy decision making.},
