Pages = {2721-2726},
Note = {4th International Conference on Probabilistic Safety Assessment and
   Management, NEW YORK, NY, SEP 13-18, 1998},
Organization = {Int Assoc Probabilist Safety Assessment \& Management; Amer Inst Chem
   Engineers; Amer Nucl Soc; Amer Soc Civil Engineers; Amer Soc Mech
   Engineers; Atom Energy Soc Japan; European Nucl Soc; European Safety \&
   Reliabil Assoc; Fed Aviat Adm; Human Factors Soc; Inst Elect \& Electr
   Engineers; Int Atom Energy Agcy; European Union, Joint Res Ctr; NASA;
   Org Econ Cooperat \& Dev; Soc Risk Analy; Syst Safety Soc; UK Safety \&
   Reliabil Soc; US Coast Guard; US DOE; US Nucl Regulatory Commiss;
   Associated Univ Inc; Beta Corp Int; BOOZ ALLEN \& HAMILTON Inc;
   Brookhaven Sci Associates; ERIN Engn \& Res Inc; PLG Inc; Sci Applicat
   Int Corp; SCIENTECH INC; Univ Maryland, Ctr Technol Risk Stud},
Abstract = {Successful implementation of a national research agenda for managing
   risk in various areas presupposes an understanding of how we as a nation
   and a culture understand risk, our associated (un)willingness to make
   social investments in risk management activities, and the institutional
   forms such risk management action will take. The emergence of what
   Ulrich Beck and others have termed the `risk society' posits a world in
   which a growing awareness of and concern for self-imperilment and the
   destruction of nature is beginning to change the understanding of risk
   itself from a purely technical term to a socially defined state. This
   paper discusses how our cultural understanding of risk (which includes
   but is not limited to a technical component) is translated into public
   policy. It also discusses the roles public perception, government and
   industry action, and scientific findings play in risk management, and
   the changing, relative importance of these sectors over time. The
   primary argument of this paper is that defining `risk' in
   technologically advanced societies today is not only about scientific
   calculations of probabilities and consequences, but about choices among
   various social futures. Risk management is about the ability to project
   potential futures, to choose that which is most desirable, and to
   construct an appropriate plan of social action that will incorporate but
   not be limited to scientific information on risk. Any research program
   that addresses risk must recognize, therefore, a moral and political
   dimension to ensure effective utilization of research results in social
   decision-making.},
