Year = {2013},
Note = {13th International Conference on Environmental Science and Technology
   (CEST), Athens, GREECE, SEP 05-07, 2013},
Organization = {Univ Aegean; Global Network Environm Sci \& Technol},
Abstract = {The annual extent of the seasonal hypoxic zone that forms in the
   northern Gulf of Mexico, which is currently the largest in the western
   Atlantic Ocean, has been documented since the mid-1980s. A 2007 USEPA
   science reassessment concluded that nitrogen (N) and phosphorus (P)
   loads discharged from the Mississippi-Atchafalaya River Basin (MARB) are
   the primary cause of the Northern Gulf of Mexico hypoxic zone. The
   primary MARB source regions of nutrients exported to the Gulf of Mexico
   have been documented to be the 492,000 km2 Upper Mississippi River Basin
   (UMRB) and the 525,770 km2 Ohio Tennessee River Basin (OTRB).
   Agricultural nonpoint source pollution is the main source of N and P
   discharged to the UMRB and ORTB stream systems, primarily via fertilizer
   and/or livestock manure applied to cropland or pasture, although urban
   areas and point sources are also important nutrient sources. To address
   these issues, we are constructing an integrated modelling system that
   will: (1) estimate nutrient loads from the UMRB and ORTB regions for
   different scenarios, (2) route the estimated nutrient loads for each
   scenario to the Gulf of Mexico, and (3) estimate the resultant size of
   the seasonal hypoxic zone using a hypoxic zone regression model. The
   Soil and Water Assessment Tool (SWAT) water quality model is being used
   to simulate the land management schemes in the URMB and ORTB and
   resultant nutrient loads to the Gulf of Mexico. This SWAT application
   incorporates a greatly refined subwatershed structure relative to
   previous SWAT applications for the study regions. The placement of
   alternative cropping systems and/or management practices on specific
   landscapes within SWAT for the two regions will be performed primarily
   via an interface with an evolutionary algorithm (EA) and corresponding
   cost data for each management system. The hypoxic zone size is estimated
   as a function of nutrient fluxes discharged from the Mississippi River
   and other relevant data. An overview of the entire system is presented
   here, including descriptions of each major component and core land use,
   soil, climate, topographic and other data inputs required for both SWAT
   models. Initial calibration and validation results for the study region
   will be described in the companion Part 2 study.},
