@inproceedings{ WOS:000072818300005,
Author = {Franz, TP and Gregor, DJ and Eisenreich, SJ},
Editor = {Baker, JE},
Title = {Snow deposition of atmospheric semivolatile organic chemicals},
Booktitle = {ATMOSPHERIC DEPOSITION OF CONTAMINANTS TO THE GREAT LAKES AND COASTAL
   WATERS},
Series = {SETAC TECHNICAL PUBLICATIONS SERIES},
Year = {1997},
Pages = {73-107},
Note = {Session on Atmospheric Deposition of Contaminants to the Great Lakes and
   Coastal Waters, at the SETAC 15th Meeting, DENVER, CO, OCT 30-NOV 03,
   1994},
Organization = {Soc Environm Toxicol \& Chem; SETAC Fdn Environm Educ},
Abstract = {Concern over the dietary intake of hazardous chemicals by indigenous
   peoples in Arctic regions has stimulated recent investigations into the
   global transport of anthropogenic emissions from the industrialized
   mid-latitudes to polar regions {[}1-9]. The process by which atmospheric
   compounds are transported north and, upon encountering colder
   temperatures, are preferentially removed from the atmosphere has been
   variously termed ``global distillation{''} {[}10], ``cold trap
   condensation{''} {[}11-13], and ``global fractionation and cold
   condensation{''} {[}6]. Cold temperature global focusing of
   anthropogenic emissions is stimulated by the reduction of an organic
   chemical's vapor pressure which consequently enhances gas/particle
   partitioning and subsequent deposition of particles by wet and dry
   processes. Similarly, cold winter and polar temperatures may intensify
   vapor phase adsorption/partitioning to terrestrial plant or snow
   surfaces {[}14-16]. These phenomena may cause polar regions to become
   sinks for semivolatile organic chemicals (SOCs), such as PCBs, PAHs,
   PCDDs and PCDFs, and some chlorinated agricultural pesticides (Table
   5-1).
   Recent reviews of contaminant levels in the Arctic have summarized the
   current status of Arctic pollution, suspected source regions, and
   dominant atmospheric and oceanic transfer pathways {[}17-19]. The
   potential for accumulation of anthropogenic chemicals in the Arctic
   prompted the eight countries bordering the Arctic and six others to form
   the International Arctic Science Committee (IASC). Established in 1990,
   the goal of this organization is to coordinate joint research projects
   in the Arctic {[}20]. At a June 1994 workshop sponsored by the Canadian
   government, an international group of scientists addressed research
   issues, formulated strategies, and presented guidelines for
   investigating the behavior and fate of SOCs in northern environments
   {[}21]. A widely dispersed monitoring network has been established in
   the Canadian and Russian Arctic to evaluate the exposure of the polar
   region to anthropogenic emissions from the populated mid-latitudes of
   the northern hemisphere {[}22,23].
   Since a significant proportion of annual precipitation occurs as snow in
   northern latitudes, deposition of atmospheric contaminants by snow may
   contribute substantially to annual loadings, yet has often been
   overlooked as a unique medium for deposition of SOCs. Mass balance
   studies have based annual wet deposition estimates on rain measurements
   of SOCs, assuming snow inputs to be similar {[}24-26], while others have
   based winter Loadings of SOCs on a single sample of the snowpack
   {[}27,28] or on biweekly measurements of precipitation using heated rain
   samplers {[}29-32]. Because of the obvious differences in the physical
   nature of the hydrometeor, solid versus liquid, the mechanisms and
   efficiencies of snow scavenging of atmospheric SOCs may be distinctly
   different from rain scavenging. Although snowpacks accumulate SOCs from
   both wet and dry deposition processes over the winter, they may also
   experience losses from volatilization or meltwater percolation and
   therefore underestimate total deposition. Rain samplers are not only
   inefficient at collecting snow, but the heated surface may cause some of
   the more volatile SOCs to evaporate during melting. Thus, the strategies
   presently utilized to measure winter deposition of SOCs have serious
   limitations.
   The goal of this chapter is to summarize our present understanding of
   the environmental behavior and fate of SOCs in snow and the techniques
   employed to study these processes. The specific processes discussed
   include the scavenging of atmospheric contaminants by snow and the
   accumulation and retention of organic pollutants within the snowpack.},
